o generating 3d cell culture models  that are in development so a lot of  technology here so a lot of different  approaches and sometimes one approach is  more favorable than another for getting  different cell subtypes to aggregate so  one of the biggest challenges especially  when you use primary cells is actually  getting cells to a great sometimes you  get in the picture on the far left here  just little micro aggregates of only a  few cells sometimes they're very  irregularly shaped as you can see on the  far right but what we're typically  aiming here  for here is a nice spheroidal  aggregation as you'll see in the center  now when you're looking to evaluate  these 3d cell culture models you know  you run your guesses maybe you're  screening compounds whether it's for  efficacy or toxicity there's a couple of  different ways that you can interrogate  them right you can use some standard  biochemical analyses and different types  of assays pretty low cost most times the  throughput can be high depending on the  type of assay that you're looking to run  but a lot of the time you're not getting  any spatial or cellular information  similarly you can dissolve the steroid  and use immuno labeling based approaches  or other dyes and use flow cytometry to  evaluate your cells so you get some  cellular information here but again  you're losing this spatial organization  of that larger 3d structure so the next  step up in complexity and thus a little  bit more expensive as well is to use  wide field imaging however this will  only allow you to really characterize  the cells at the periphery of your 3d  cell culture models just because that  your objects are limited in terms of  being able to actually get good new  resolution you're allowing so much out  of plain light in that you're not going  to be allowed to actually see each  individual region of your 3d cell  culture model as you image through it so  in order to get a little bit more  perspective on the different regions of  3d cell culture models a lot of folks  will use histopathology so you can think  of this from larger tissue scale we are  suctioning larger tissues into a couple  of micron slices a similar approach can  be taken using steroids but you can  imagine if a spheroid is only 100 200  300 microns this histopathology approach  becomes very tedious and very low  throughput it's very difficult to get a  steroid evenly bisected using  histopathology and then to get repeat  sections in order to get some 3d context  there so ultimately one of the really  nice approaches is to use can focal  microscopy you're able to image deep  into the tissue  and really only be visualizing a few  cell layers at a time so the cost does  go up the cost per well in order to  conduct this sort of analyses just  because the time that's required and  thus that the throughput is a bit lower  but you get a lot more information out  of it here one of the challenges though  about using good focal microscopy is  that you are sort of limited in depth so  you can see from the image on the right  the top two rows here we have a steroid  and you're going if you're going from  left to right and continuing on the  bottom row here you can see that as you  image deeper and deeper into the  spheroid you sort of start to lose the  cells at the centre of this here right  and the reason for this is that all of  the the individual cells and their  components are sort of acting at like  lenses and diffracting the light as it  passes through each cell layer  especially when you get to the center of  the zero and you really you're losing a  lot of light and thus you're not able to  see those cells that are at the center  so we've developed a number of tissue  clearing reagents these are commercially  available through the remote Fisher  under the name Saito Vista and you can  see that if you now take that same see  writings cleared it and you look at that  z stack going deeper and deeper in Z now  you can actually see all of the cells  throughout the entire depth of the 3d  cell culture model and this of course  allows us to see more total cells so you  can see her from this graph as we're  going through every Z slice we're  getting many more cells that are  actually able to be imaged and again the  reason for this is the ability of the  light to actually pass through the  entire cell so the clearing reagents are  a refractive index matching solution  such that that incident light no longer  gets scattered instead it's able to pass  directly through these 3d cell culture  models so by allowing for that more  complete characterization you can think  of things like you know if there's some  sort of heterogeneous distribution of  how cells are responding to a  therapeutic now you're able to much more  effectively characterize that rather  than  characterizing the cells on the exterior  surface so I wanted to dive into an  example here of an important application  of 3d cell culture models this is liver  toxicity and disease so we've developed  at visit call a number of 3d cell  culture models in order to study both  liver toxicity and disney's these range  from either mono cultures of just  hepatocyte or have our G cells to more  complex co-cultures with other  supporting cell types so in the model  that I'll talk most about today we've  used HEPA RG cells which are in  immortalized hepatocyte line since the  hepatocyte progenitor and these have  been Co cultured with a mix of non  parenchymal cells derived from human  liver  so those non parenchymal cells sort of  support the liver function in a number  of ways so I'll go through briefly here  each of the cell types that's sort of  featured in this model of course we have  the hepatocyte like cells these produce  bile they sort of function to metabolize  a lot of drugs they store glycogen and  lipids and then comprise about 70 to 80  percent of the liver liver sinusoidal  endothelial cells here are labeled with  seeming 31 they form this sort of highly  fenestrated sinusoid network that runs  through the liver and really facilitates  that blood filtration capacity of the  liver then we have kupffer cells these  are sort of macrophage like cells they  patrol those liver sinusoids and the  incite an inflammatory response when  appropriate  there are stellate cells when these guys  become activated in a lot of disease  contexts they actually started producing  a lot of extracellular matrix which can  lead to fibrosis especially in the  context of some diseases we'll talk  about in a few minutes here and then  there's some other supporting cell  subtypes here I'm highlighting calandria  sites these form sort of these bile duct  structures that help transport file  through  deliver and out of the liver and there's  a number of other cells that we haven't  really talked you about here that are  derived from this non Frank molt  population but altogether these liver 3d  cell culture models an able to enable us  to ask a number of different questions  so one of the first things that we've  looked at using these models is  drug-induced liver injury the ability to  evaluate toxicity in in resulting from  different types of therapeutics so in  this case we dosed those heavier gene on  parenchymal co-culture models with  increasing doses of acetaminophen in  order to demonstrate this principle of  using imaging to evaluate toxicity in  these 3d cell culture models so the 3d  cell culture models have been labeled  with both a viability indicator here  shown in green and a mitochondrial  health indicator so the viability  indicator works by exclusion so a  compromised membrane allows the  viability indicator in and it gets fixed  in place so the more green you see the  more dead cells and you can see that as  we increase the dose of acetaminophen  will get more dead cells but what's  really nice about imaging is you can  start to layer some of these different  assays on top of one another to start  start to understand a mechanism behind  toxicity or therapeutic efficacy so in  this case you used a mitochondrial  membrane potential indicator because  it's supposed that if a drug is  cholestatic in its mechanism of  drug-induced liver injury you actually  get a cumulation of bile acid that can  cause damage to the mitochondria so here  we do see there is some of that effect  here with the dosing with acetaminophen  such that we see a loss of mitochondrial  membrane potential with increasing dose  of acetaminophen and to highlight  another aspect of these three cell  culture models the inclusion of immune  cell subtypes and not just liver 3d cell  culture models but a number of 3ds  our models has become increasingly  recognized as important to effectively  screening different types of  therapeutics for either toxicity or or  efficacy so in this case we've dosed  with trove ofloxacin with and without  stimulation with lipopolysaccharide and  we see that we get a pretty dramatic  shift in the dose response curve when we  either stimulate or don't stimulate  presumably due to the immune component  that's present within these 3d cell  culture models in addition to these  imaging based approaches we can also  layer on top a number of other non  imaging based approaches a lot of the  time using the same spheroid well so you  can multiplex for five different colors  within a single spheroid but then you  can also take self ultra supernatants  and analyze those for things like bile  acid secretion LDH release we can look  at reactive metabolites urea production  the list goes on so it's a really nice  way to evaluate a number of different  endpoints especially when you might have  this increased relevancy afforded by 3d  cell culture models and the last thing I  wanted to talk about with these 3d cell  culture models particularly in the liver  context is liver disease so not just  screening toxicity but more complex  processes that maybe can't quite be  screened in 2d so non-alcoholic fatty  liver disease is growing in prevalence  in the United States and across the  world really and it sort of starts with  this this abnormal lipid accumulation  and as those lipid droplets continue to  accumulate you get increased  inflammation that inflammation  eventually activates the scaly cells  that we talked about a bit earlier and  leads to deposition of a lot of  extracellular matrix and this is termed  fibrosis and this ultimately leads to  sort of an advanced cell damaged state  and a lot of scarring but impairs liver  functionality so there's currently no  approved therapeutics for to address  that end-stage fibrotic state so a lot  of folks are looking  to evaluate these compounds but the  current 2d assays don't really do such a  good job of evaluating compounds in this  arena we started out with span ptosis  that accumulation of lipid in in the  liver here we used a much more  simplistic 3d cell culture model and I  just wanted to call attention to that so  we used have g2 cells which is an  immortalized liver carcinoma cell line  and the reason being is they can be much  a sort of more high-throughput just  because you can continue to propagate  these cells in culture without the need  to continually you know resaw primary  material and worry about the lots a lot  variation there so this sort of brings  down the cost of this assay at the  expense of of the sort of later stage  evaluation of more advanced stages of  the disease but it allows us to get a  good approximation at least if you're  looking to do some side-by-side  comparisons so it's always good to think  about what's the most important endpoint  in this phase of the assay and you can  always continue to build on that and  really optimize what you're looking for  in terms of cost throughput and in vivo  relevancy so here we use these have to  choose your eyes we go some with  increasing concentrations of oleic acid  and you see that the amount of lipid per  cell does in fact exhibit this dose  response that we'd expect to see again  we can multiplex this with some  different endpoints not just these  imaging based endpoints we can look at  things like tnf-alpha secretion so you  can start to get a sense of that next  step that inflammatory step but  ultimately if you're looking to evaluate  disease in this case fibrosis those  jelly cells that I mentioned earlier are  really the key to allowing these the the  recapitulation of that disease state  there are the guys that are gonna get  activated and deposit ECM so we really  have to be a part of the model in order  to recapitulate that disease most  effectively so here we took the same  that heavy our gene on  Malco culture based approach treated  with tgf-beta in order to stimulate  fibrosis and looked at a number of  different imaging based readouts so we  looked at activation of stellate cells  here shown in green these two green  markers pdgfr beta and alpha SMA and we  see that we get a great increase in the  amount of stelae cell activation when we  stimulate with tgf-beta likewise those  stellate cells are they start to deposit  collagen here labeled either collagen  one or pan collagen to label multiple  college and subtypes at once and we see  that we do get increased deposition of  collagen as well you have been a number  of proposed approaches to ameliorate  this fibrotic state one of which is to  treat with some sort of al v inhibitor  so simple therapeutic if you will that  we've used here isn't how v inhibitor  and we see that it does ameliorate  fibrosis to a pretty significant extent  similarly we can multiplex these with  other non imaging base tosses to look at  things like cytokine production alt and  ast you know commonly used metrics to  evaluate liver functionality and we  again see that we stimulate with  tgf-beta you get some sort of fold  increase in these different cytokine  readouts but when you treat with an OP v  inhibitor we get that amelioration of  the disease state so that's just one  example of using 3d cell culture assays  in order to sort of study a disease more  effectively but there's a number of  other examples out there for this 3d  advantage if you're looking at  evaluating drug delivery approaches how  well a therapeutic you can penetrate a  solid tissue of course you need some  sort of solid tissue in order to  evaluate you can't just treat a mono  layer of cells so here I have a couple  images on the top left of a steroid  bottle that we've used to evaluate drug  delivery in a 3d cell culture model then  on the right here you see evaluating  selim filtration so very similar to  evaluating drug delivery as folks move  more and more towards these cellular  based approaches to treating disease or  evaluating the immune component of  different diseases understanding cell  infiltration in particularly immune cell  infiltration becomes an important  question so by using 3d cell culture  models we're actually able to do that  again if you just have cells on a 2d  mono layer you can't really look at what  they're infiltrating and some of these  studies can be conducted using excised  tissues it just becomes a lot more low  throughput so having this ability to  recapitulate that that state whether  it's a disease State or the treatment  state using a 3d cell culture model is a  really powerful tool and then on the  bottom left here I've given sort of a  slightly different take on the 3d  approach evaluating drug permeability in  a blood-brain barrier model it's sort of  a less 3d 3d cell culture model but you  do still have some sort of 3d context of  looking at the transport of some sort of  material in a 3d context rather than an  in the cellular based model rather than  in a traditional approach there's many  many other applications of this  advantage of 3d cell culture models and  acids and I hope you take the  opportunity to explore them a bit be  happy to take any questions and I look  forward to following up with each one of  you on any questions that you may have
 

